## Goal
The commit adds a per-run token and session cookie for the UI and API of `serve`, plus a path-browser warning (DEV-24 security). The session slice, though, covers something else: disabling the Email notification channel in GitHub notification settings through browser automation (orca), to be checked afterward.

## Problems
- Element refs from orca snapshots kept going stale ("Unknown ref"), so clicks failed.
- The tab sometimes dropped to about:blank with no buttons, so selectors found nothing.
- The settings dialog had no checkboxes. It uses aria-selected items, so the checkbox-based script found nothing.
- Pointer/click sequences by eval did not open the dialog until the page was reloaded.

## Decision
The agent closed the broken tab and reopened the settings page. It then reloaded the page before each attempt and drove the buttons through eval with waits, which opened the dialog. It next proposed re-reading the aria-selected state and unchecking Email, but the user rejected that call. The user will change the settings by hand, and the agent will only verify the result afterward. This keeps account-setting changes under the user's control.

## Rejected
- Clicking by snapshot ref: refs went stale.
- Clicking by element id selector: the id was not found.
- Automated toggling of Email by the agent: the user declined and chose to do it manually.

## Touched
orca (browser automation CLI), GitHub notification settings page, serve (UI/API token and session cookie, per the commit message)
